ng hall's time murders  spiked in dallas as they did across the  country but according to numbers from  the dallas police department her  detectives solved more than 70 percent  of homicides that's 20 points higher  than the national average one of the  first things my team and i did we met  and i told them i don't want to go to  any community where we have great  relationships i only want to go to those  communities where our relationships are  challenged i started there and then hall  set about broadening the diversity in  her own rank and file we went from 17 18  uh latinos hispanics in the police  department under my leadership to about  24 percent because the largest portion  of our community we're hispanic i want  to show you if you don't mind we have um  the rory norman news conference we  showed hall a press conference she held  in 2020. not only are we broken-hearted  we are angry after gunfire killed a  one-year-old named rory norman in his  own bedroom  what's that about for you  i'd gone to the hospital and saw  his body  very small body  and it was  infuriating  and this  has to stop in this city we checked with  the dallas police department and two  years later there's still been no  arrests made in the murder of rory  norman  it's disheartening  how can that be  you know  if if i had that answer  i'd be rich  there's some brokenness  in the amount of guns that are on our  streets  there's some brokenness in the  relationships between police and the  community and so what are we going to do  about it at this point it takes  work and it takes  owning  where we are flawed  fixing it  complicated problem and fixing it is a  complicated formula hall told us police  departments need more resources more  technology and greater buy-in from the  communities where there is now mistrust  and only then she says can we even think  about starting to reverse  this half-century long trend  but does she seem hope it seems you hear  that and it's like well that's not going  to happen the the thing is  what she has done or did when she was  chief of police in dallas it hasn't been  seen everywhere she began to broaden the  ranks in a way that mirrored the makeup  the diversity of the community that that  she was in charge of policing and she  had tangible numbers that would back up  that approach so part of what needs to  be done is rooted  in making sure that the folks who are  protecting the community reflect that  community are a part of that community  and is that something that can be  replicated in other cities well she says  if we're going to have any kind of  chance at restoring the trust that's  been eroded there must be you have to  yeah well this has been an incredible  series you've been doing it all week and  many of our stations shout out to  producer andy best who is really driving  the train on so much of the research the  team and of course the broad cooperation  between the cbs network and the local  cbs stations well for more on this  reporting watch crime without punishment  fighting for justice on the cbs news  stream tonight at 8 pm eastern or on  demand anytime on paramount plus or by  downloading the free cbs news app
 

